nsliteration (zero-based)","sourceId":"terms"}],"definition":"System of Pronoun Forms","id":"terms.t0.r9.sense1","status":"attested"}],"status":"attested","variants":[{"note":"Verbatim script cell","script":"archived script slots","sourceFonts":["transliteration 5"],"text":"Meglast\u00d7"},{"note":"Verbatim transliteration cell; not IPA","script":"archived transliteration slots","sourceFonts":["test seven"],"text":"Meglast\u00d7"}]},"38d093290c412e85f17e83ef7bec66598e96531309d2dbe654529234fc99f162":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 26; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"distant","tense":"future"},"form":"rat","id":"hadthem.future-distant","kind":"inflection","label":"future-distant","notes":"Future table and paired script/transliteration example rows disagree; no winner selected.","status":"disputed"},"8dc24c74faf8f8949df13b4249b76bfeab57d3099ff3d763e645ae09c3e3cd65":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 13; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"agnostic","tense":"future"},"form":"at","id":"hadthem.future-general","kind":"inflection","label":"future-general","notes":"","status":"attested"},"6a869cda3b408ea10591dbec43185993364dced88e9abd9170eea87e7b4bc706":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 23; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"immediate","tense":"future"},"form":"dat","id":"hadthem.future-immediate","kind":"inflection","label":"future-immediate","notes":"Future table and paired script/transliteration example rows disagree; no winner selected.","status":"disputed"},"4efdf7f7504da7092719b9cfe20c4715b0bcbf0cb331daf8c69b87cf678c660b":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 16; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"distant","tense":"past"},"form":"diz","id":"hadthem.past-distant","kind":"inflection","label":"past-distant","notes":"","status":"attested"},"edf091b452f2c73a724a4c1903e928390b3e024e4bb3cc5be9e53dd9a569b4f0":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 6; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"agnostic","tense":"past"},"form":"iz","id":"hadthem.past-general","kind":"inflection","label":"past-general","notes":"","status":"attested"},"bd113085881b49e71dc6c882bb086d3d551d270ed87988e2c2350f344c3a340e":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 19; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"immediate","tense":"past"},"form":"riz","id":"hadthem.past-immediate","kind":"inflection","label":"past-immediate","notes":"","status":"attested"},"39ee11dd1b47bebdd515cab9a5d038a46fdd41640ad84a0cb7c70ad3fe38a08a":{"cells":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraph 10; examples 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"entryIds":[],"features":{"distance":"present","tense":"present"},"form":"ow","id":"hadthem.present","kind":"inflection","label":"present","notes":"","status":"attested"},"3ed3ebe0888b23eed5ed6de4db1e8884137455d6c6aa3f9c1096bb6a006b37db":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"correlative-chart"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.correlatives","label":"Correlatives, negation and open questions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"The correlative chart distinguishes action, quality, reason, time, location, manner, quantity, human association, thing identity and truth. Definitive and indefinite prefixes and an interrogative pattern are visible; several columns are blank. Retain the gaps. The polished clause supplies a causative conjunction. Internal/external negation and passive derivations need further source alignment before productive generalization.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"4901c87be0627764fb07efc61e88289810ccf0af66583ec0cbf95f735ee78d56":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"chart-1"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.lexicon","label":"Dictionary policy and frequency","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Keep original chart entries and stable sense IDs. Proposed current spellings are separate records with source links. Prefer attested roots and transparent inflections over randomly generated syllables. Measure running-text token frequencies separately from dictionary type counts and historical font slots. The tiny surviving corpus cannot establish population frequencies or justify smoothing away rare letters.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"8b2241554c4a6f7c99dbac00bc4080f02291b675cf0046b342eff124d55ca02f":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.nominals","label":"Nominal classes and modifiers","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Retain noun classes and overt conversion clitics. Use the polished thing determiner with count nouns and the quality determiner with beauty; -get marks the object. Count-noun modifiers follow the noun and take the attested count-modifier clitic. The clitic chart distinguishes mass, count, collective and title nouns; do not collapse those distinctions into English articles.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"bb9ed124ad3d080c1d5923c3f1240fb6426339d15fc2da43a3ed50941318c24f":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.order","label":"Clause order and case","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Use object/predicate\u2013subject\u2013verb as the neutral order in this draft. Noun direct objects take -get; lexical subjects take -l\u00c4y (chart-2 slots l\u00c9y). Pronouns use their subjective forms. This generalizes the polished examples; it is not proof that other orders are impossible.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"e8dba292ee39fedb19c61b50dfe831ac9a70d1233267d2716579284c5f4f5b25":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"mapping-79"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.orthography","label":"Encoding and sound","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Thimfora 79 chart 2 is the adopted display inventory: 47 letter identities with maxima/minima. Historical font slots are not Unicode phonetic values. Adapt U+00BE to U+2019 only in the review edition as in the approved page. Venik spellings used in the demo are mapped by named letter correspondence. Unresolved readings remain unknown; no invented IPA, stress rules or phonotactic constraints.","status":"draft","topic":"phonology"},"6b2bb1d1d07b74c00ab3a772753964c13b1e8c4c4fefa24b08f5b049a5975a90":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"pronoun-chart"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.pronouns","label":"Collective we and pronoun paradigms","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Interpret the attested we(singular) as a collective singular speaker group for this branch. The pronoun chart explicitly distinguishes we(pr), we(d), we(s); their exact pragmatic scope is unresolved. Use only I, collective we and inanimate singular it in the first novel test. Do not infer the complete person/number paradigm from these three forms.","status":"draft","topic":"pronouns"},"74a144175a193b747c7064fc9523f6efa8cf1474266ffe801c7a782e297625d6":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M7 source review and explicit branch proposal","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"forge.rule.tense","label":"Tense and aspect","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Verb = root + tense + aspect. Adopt -iz general past, -ow present, -at general future, -diz distant past, -riz immediate past, -dat immediate future, -rat distant future. Prefer the prose table and transliteration examples for the conflicting future pair. Use -ya simple, -am progressive, -fu perfect. The chart also lists compound aspect contractions; these remain under review. Bare infinitives are licensed only as dictionary citation forms in this draft.","status":"draft","topic":"tense"},"06f4a413d219ac85e4bf561fa1a88137ceed16e4487993636a50d2ae1ea7a0d4":{"alternatives":[{"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 23, 26, 58\u201359, 62\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"statement":"Immediate/distant future table: -dat / -rat. Script examples: -rat / -dat. Transliteration examples: -dat / -rat. Retain all readings pending adjudication.","status":"disputed"}],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 1\u20132 and 58\u201363","sourceId":"hadthem"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"hadthem.tense","label":"Tense and aspect ordering","morphologyIds":["hadthem.past-general","hadthem.present","hadthem.future-general","hadthem.past-distant","hadthem.past-immediate","hadthem.future-immediate","hadthem.future-distant"],"notes":"","statement":"Tense is indicated seperately from aspect in Vayrshemon. There are seven forms for each verb that communicate tense. Three indicate the past, one indicates the present and three indicate the future. Both the past and the future tense, unlike the present tense, can convey a range of temporal immediacy. An action is specified to have occured in a contextually dependant relative time frame. The frame of refernce for an action\u00bes tense is that which is considered normal for the action. (The distant past with regard to a geological process is distinct from the distant past with regard to a human life span or from a nuclear process like the decay of a specific radioactive isotope.) In this way an event that occured 10 years ago with regard to the invention of a micro electronic device may be described in the distant past tense but an event that occured 10 years ago with regard to the development of technology in western societies may be described in the immediate past tense even though both examples refer to the same event. \nThe general form of the past and future tense is agnostic of temporal immediacy and simply conveys that an event does not occur in the present but that it occurs sometime in the past or sometime in the future. Greater specificity is possible by using the immediate or distant variant of the past and future tense. Tense is affixed before aspect and either of the two inflections can be absent but not both. ","status":"disputed","topic":"tense"},"d3fbbd5d160a96fa175fc715d1747515afea6792b134b23a4673e52a29a5316e":{"alternatives":[{"citations":[],"statement":"Retain full marking for deliberate anaphora, solemnity or ambiguity avoidance.","status":"draft"},{"citations":[],"statement":"Reject these proposals if human reading finds the short forms unclear.","status":"draft"}],"citations":[],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m10.rule.discourse","label":"Optional classification in discourse","morphologyIds":[],"notes":" M11 metadata correction: M10 described this as a proposal but inherited an attested default. It is not attested; the earlier frozen record remains inspectable.","statement":"NEW PROPOSAL for literary register: the full thing-class form remains available in every context. Optionally suppress only its outer class marker on an established, noncontrast subject/object; on a neutral predicative nominal; or on a continuing same-class nominal subject in a coordinated enumeration. First items, new subject/object referents and contrast focus retain full marking. Case and ef\u2019 event nominalization remain. These conditions supplement the M8 full-form paradigm; they are not attested facts or a universal deletion rule.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"2d0f3a708a36edee8669d5d642d47c38c260fdb1265fb47eb420ebad981fa281":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m11.rule.maxima","label":"Maxima at sentence initials","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Use the paired Maxima letter at each mapped sentence initial. Preserve interior spelling, clitics, roots and suffixes. Physical line wrapping never introduces capitals. Letter identities come from the chart; this explicit prose policy is a proposal informed by the archived capitalized sentence openings, not a recovered complete capitalization grammar.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"b248589f72dc24029a53742f65698c08c4ea593baf01d00af2ce2491eb0c89a8":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m11.rule.topic","label":"Established subject as topic","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"In literary narrative, an established, noncontrast subject topic may precede its nominal predicate and final verb. A predicate-associated only adverb remains with the predicate. Retain the earlier predicate-first construction as an available alternative; do not shuffle clauses for variety. Case endings remain authoritative. This is a new discourse proposal, not attested word-order evidence.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"1770154dcc4071a8a910f55fd412f151102aa7a3a60f3037751100b28f23c101":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m12.rule.continuity","label":"Optional continuous subject","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"A literary continuation may leave its immediately preceding explicit subject unspoken when the same lexical participant remains established and noncontrast. Preserve predicate, adverb scope and finite tense. Use an explicit subject at a topic change, after an interruption, for contrast or when reference is unclear. Full subject clauses remain available. This is a new proposal, not an attested rule.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"371d16761f1a5563b707da95609b5f3b11b18f6676036a33093eb3dc833478e0":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M8 literary extrapolation","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m8.rule.metaphor","label":"Tempest compounds and semantic choices","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Lexicalize the proposed cloud-tower, beauty-building, belief-building, earth-dweller and sleep-see-form compounds. Interpret ly\u2019 + life as collective-our life. Use sleep as the nominal agent of surround. The temporal after-play phrase and -h\u00ebth boundary are explicit new proposals. These meanings need author review; the compiler only checks declared forms.","status":"draft","topic":"other"},"369a6ea4b0e6bbd5aa82f56856b236d9e09dbc38ad6083f6fa8f449ab8f8635f":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"M8 literary extrapolation","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"m8.rule.poetic","label":"Poetic nominal classes and enumeration","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"In this literary register, lexicalized compound noun heads use the thing determiner g\u00df\u00d1et\u2019 and retain case: -get predicate/object and -l\u00c9y subject. An asyndetic list of 1\u20138 nominative subjects shares the final verb. Number is generic collective here. This does not license arbitrary English word order. Existing explicit-class constructions remain available.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"31de8267d95ca7998470b058e95e240b025cd4f211615daff632a567c0b20dc2":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 2 and 19","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":["polished.1","polished.2"],"id":"polished.construction","label":"Nominal conversions and clause construction","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Retain the complete source glosses, including [b>n], [v>n], case labels, passive/perfect and modifier descriptions. Unsegmented forms remain unresolved rather than receiving guessed morpheme boundaries.","status":"uncertain","topic":"construction"},"836ad24ffa60346671c535a0293faa39a6968932c145be5bdedd7a723f7b6136":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 0, 2, 17, 19","sourceId":"polished"}],"exampleIds":["polished.1","polished.2"],"id":"polished.pronouns","label":"Pronouns attested in the polished gloss","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"The archived gloss explicitly identifies I(singular), we(singular), and it(inanimate, singular, subjective). The first two have token-level gloss alignment in polished.1; no full pronoun paradigm is inferred.","status":"attested","topic":"pronouns"},"2f56bf9fc20d547dd53433ec21aa31b6f95079399630be592d9b84f1d30c8483":{"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 0, 2, 4 (zero-based)","sourceId":"polished"}],"id":"polished.1","label":"Polished example 1","notes":"","offsetUnit":"unicode-scalar","orthographyId":null,"rawGloss":"[b>n]\u00beMany-accusative I(singular) be(now,simple). One-accusative we(singular) be(now,simple).","sourceFonts":["test seven","transliteration 5"],"status":"attested","text":"G\u00dd\u00bem\u00dfvget r\u00e1 r\u00d7owya. Neksusget ly r\u00d7owya.","tokens":[{"end":9,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["[b>n]\u00b7Many-accusative"],"morphemes":["G\u00dd\u00bem\u00dfvget"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":0,"status":"attested","surface":"G\u00dd\u00bem\u00dfvget"},{"end":12,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["I(singular)"],"morphemes":["r\u00e1"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":10,"status":"attested","surface":"r\u00e1"},{"end":19,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["be(now,simple)"],"morphemes":["r\u00d7owya"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":13,"status":"attested","surface":"r\u00d7owya"},{"end":30,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["One-accusative"],"morphemes":["Neksusget"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":21,"status":"attested","surface":"Neksusget"},{"end":33,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["we(singular)"],"morphemes":["ly"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":31,"status":"attested","surface":"ly"},{"end":40,"entryIds":[],"glosses":["be(now,simple)"],"morphemes":["r\u00d7owya"],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":34,"status":"attested","surface":"r\u00d7owya"}],"translation":"I am many. We are one.","transliteration":"G\u00dd\u00bem\u00dfvget r\u00e1 r\u00d7owya. Neksusget ly r\u00d7owya."},"741d2ffb0e4f45bf0c7742b1806a207a910839181518a0285086c7d8322fc6a2":{"citations":[{"locator":"paragraphs 17, 19, 21 (zero-based)","sourceId":"polished"}],"id":"polished.2","label":"Polished example 2","notes":"","offsetUnit":"unicode-scalar","orthographyId":null,"rawGloss":"A(thing)\u00be[v>n]\u00bewonder+accusative a(thing)\u00bejewel+nominative blue\u00be[dofn, count.] adorn(passive, perfect) light withpostposition<1by means of>, surround(passive, perfect) only+[bofv] night [v>dofn, count]\u00becease+internal negation(d) bypostposition <1designates agent>, be(now, simple) for<1causative conjunction> a(quality)\u00bebeauty+accusative fragile+[dofn, count] it(inanimate, singular, subjective) possess(now, simple) true+[bofv].","sourceFonts":["test seven","transliteration 5"],"status":"attested","text":"G\u00df\u00d1et\u00beef\u00beh\u00c9ft\u00cdget g\u00df\u00d1et\u00beyeml\u00c9y \u00c7\u00d9y\u00bem\u00e7 \u00e7\u00db\u00c9fu b\u00d7\u00c7r r\u00c9n\u00d3, o\u00c3t\u00e7fu yib\u00be\u00ebw \u00c5\u00d9gif \u00d1n\u00e7\u00befusyk \u00cbers, r\u00d7owya kenst g\u00dfnot\u00beel\u00ebinget g\u00e5his\u00bem\u00e7 po \u00d1\u00c5zowya t\u00d7lom\u00be\u00ebw.","tokens":[{"end":17,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":0,"status":"uncertain","surface":"G\u00df\u00d1et\u00beef\u00beh\u00c9ft\u00cdget"},{"end":30,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":18,"status":"uncertain","surface":"g\u00df\u00d1et\u00beyeml\u00c9y"},{"end":37,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":31,"status":"uncertain","surface":"\u00c7\u00d9y\u00bem\u00e7"},{"end":43,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":38,"status":"uncertain","surface":"\u00e7\u00db\u00c9fu"},{"end":48,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":44,"status":"uncertain","surface":"b\u00d7\u00c7r"},{"end":53,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":49,"status":"uncertain","surface":"r\u00c9n\u00d3"},{"end":61,"entryIds":[],"glosses":[],"morphemes":[],"morphologyIds":[],"senseIds":[],"start":55,"statu